.TH FINDBTC 1
.SH NAME
findbtc \- scan raw block devices and forensic images for cryptocurrency wallet traces
.SH SYNOPSIS
findbtc [options] TARGET [TARGET ...]
.br
.B findbtc
.B \-report
.I hits.jsonl
.br
.B findbtc \-walk
.I DIR
.br
.B findbtc \-advise
.I TARGET
.SH DESCRIPTION
findbtc scans disks, images, files, and pipes for wallet traces
(seed phrases, keys, descriptors, keystores) and, with the secrets
profile, leaked credentials. Detections print to stdout; logs,
progress, and the final [COMPLETE] line go to stderr, so
.B \-json
output stays parseable. The tool is offline by default and never
prints key or seed material without the owner\-only
.B \-\-reveal
flag.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-advise
Inspect TARGET and print the recommended scan command with reasons (never scans, never runs anything)
.TP
.B \-balance\-endpoint
Opt\-in: query ADDRESS balances from this Esplora\-compatible base URL (only with \-watch; leaks addresses to that server)
.TP
.B \-baseline
Suppress \-walk/\-patch findings fingerprinted in baseline FILE (a reviewed hits.jsonl from an earlier sweep)
.TP
.B \-case\-log
Append a JSON case\-log record per scan to FILE (source identity, streaming hashes, skipped ranges, counts)
.TP
.B \-checkpoint
Journal scan progress to FILE every 1MB for crash recovery
.TP
.B \-complete
With \-report: enumerate checksum\-valid completions for partial\-seed (near\-miss) hits (needs \-\-reveal; local terminal only)
.TP
.B \-complete\-max
With \-report \-complete: candidates shown per hit, most\-likely first (only with \-complete) (default 128)
.TP
.B \-complete\-out
With \-report \-complete: write candidate account keys (watch\-only) to PATH for \-watch (only with \-complete)
.TP
.B \-context
Bytes carved before and after each hit (only with \-extract\-dir) (default 1048576)
.TP
.B \-dfxml
Convert a \-json hits file (or \- for stdin) to DFXML on stdout instead of scanning
.TP
.B \-extract\-dir
Carve bytes around each hit into DIR/hit\-NNNNNN.bin plus a .json sidecar (never on the scanned device itself)
.TP
.B \-fail\-on\-hit
Exit 3 when the scan or report finds anything (for CI gates and pre\-commit hooks); without it, finding hits still exits 0
.TP
.B \-fs
Filesystem\-aware mode: recover deleted entries with names from the NTFS/ext/FAT/exFAT volume in FILE and scan their content
.TP
.B \-fs\-offset
Byte offset of the volume boot sector inside FILE (only with \-fs and \-unallocated\-only); when omitted, the partition table is followed automatically (default 0)
.TP
.B \-gen\-completion
Write a shell completion script for bash, zsh, or fish to stdout and exit (generated from the flag table; scan inputs still refuse)
.TP
.B \-gen\-man
Write the findbtc man page (troff) to stdout and exit (OPTIONS generated from the flag table; scan inputs still refuse)
.TP
.B \-hashes
Extract crack\-ready password hashes from FILE (or \- for stdin) instead of scanning
.TP
.B \-json
Print detections as JSON lines instead of human text
.TP
.B \-patch
Parse input as a patch series (git log \-p): attribute hits to commit+path with history fingerprints for \-baseline
.TP
.B \-profile
Detector set: empty (wallet matchers) or secrets (adds private\-key blocks and credential shapes; see docs/SECRETS_PROFILE.md)
.TP
.B \-report
Summarize a \-json hits file (or \- for stdin) instead of scanning
.TP
.B \-resume
Resume from \-checkpoint instead of scanning from the start
.TP
.B \-reveal
Print seed words for BIP39 hits (owner recovery only; NEVER share this output)
.TP
.B \-s
Start at byte offset (default 0)
.TP
.B \-salvage
Analyze FILE for salvageable database pages instead of scanning
.TP
.B \-salvage\-out
Write the salvaged database image to PATH (only with \-salvage)
.TP
.B \-targets
Read more scan targets from FILE (one path per line; blank lines and # comments ignored) in addition to positionals (flags must precede targets)
.TP
.B \-tokenlist
Build a BTCRecover tokenlist from the words in FILE, a carve, or hits.jsonl with carves (or \- for stdin) instead of scanning
.TP
.B \-tokenlist\-max
Emit at most N token lines, first\-seen first (only with \-tokenlist) (default 256)
.TP
.B \-tokenlist\-out
Write the tokenlist to PATH instead of stdout (only with \-tokenlist)
.TP
.B \-unallocated\-only
Scan only unallocated filesystem space (volume at \-fs\-offset); skips live data
.TP
.B \-verify\-case\-log
Re\-hash the sources behind each record in case\-log FILE and report match/mismatch instead of scanning
.TP
.B \-version
Print version and exit
.TP
.B \-walk
Sweep every regular file under DIR with the standard detectors (symlinks not followed unless \-walk\-follow\-symlinks)
.TP
.B \-walk\-follow\-symlinks
Follow symlinks during \-walk (loops fail safe per link)
.TP
.B \-walk\-maxdepth
Descend at most N levels below DIR during \-walk (0 = unlimited) (default 0)
.TP
.B \-watch
Derive watch\-only addresses from extended public keys in FILE (a carve, a key file, or hits.jsonl with carves) instead of scanning
.TP
.B \-watch\-count
Addresses to derive per chain, external and change (only with \-watch) (default 20)
.TP
.B \-watch\-format
Address\-list format: csv or json (only with \-watch) (default csv)
.TP
.B \-watch\-out
Write the address list to PATH instead of stdout (only with \-watch)
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The run covered its target, hits or not. A batch that scanned at
least one target exits 0 even when others failed (failures print
loudly with a WARNING).
.TP
.B 1
Runtime error or zero coverage: nothing was scanned. The reason
is on stderr.
.TP
.B 2
Bad flags or usage.
.TP
.B 3
Hits found, only with
.B \-fail\-on\-hit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan a disk to machine\-readable hits, then triage offline:
.PP
.RS
.nf
findbtc \-json /dev/sda > hits.jsonl
findbtc \-report hits.jsonl
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan several images in one run with per\-target case\-log records:
.PP
.RS
.nf
findbtc \-json \-case\-log case.jsonl img1.E01 img2.raw > hits.jsonl
.fi
.RE
